-> `∈`/`∉` as a boolean expression (e.g. inside `assert`) used to require
-> a syntactically type-shaped right side (a name starting uppercase, or
-> a quoted literal) — `1 ∈ c` where `c` is a lowercase variable holding a
-> Set failed to even parse. But T26 already treats types as sets, so a
-> variable holding a Set/Schema/Union is exactly as valid a right side as
-> a type name (T27 follow-up). This tests the general-expression form;
-> the type-name form (`x ∈ Number`) is unaffected, see is_union_check.code
-> and friends.
p ∈ Z
p > 0
p < 3
q ∈ Z
q > 3
q < 6
c = p ∪ q
assert 1 ∈ c
assert 4 ∈ c
assert 3 ∉ c

-> Membership against a Schema value (an object satisfying its fields).
k = { x ∈ {1, 2} }
assert { x = 1 } ∈ k
assert { x = 3 } ∉ k

-> Membership against a Union value (matches at least one alternative).
Status = {"Success"} ∪ { code ∈ {1, 2} }
assert "Success" ∈ Status
assert { code = 1 } ∈ Status
assert { code = 9 } ∉ Status

-> The capitalized/type-name form still works exactly as before — this
-> isn't a new code path replacing the old one, just a fallback added
-> alongside it.
assert 5 ∈ Number
assert "x" ∉ Number
